# Polyglot interop — shared kernel.
#
# The polyglot layer bridges Mezze values to guest-language objects via
# the Truffle InteropLibrary. JVM interop lives alongside this kernel in
# `std::polyglot::jvm` — Mezze always runs on the JVM, so `JvmForeign`
# ships built-in. Every additional guest language (Python, JS, Wasm, …)
# arrives via an installable provider package (`mezze-python`,
# `mezze-js`, `mezze-wasm`) declared in the project's `Mezze.toml`;
# each package defines its own opaque `<Lang>Foreign` newtype so the
# compiler prevents accidental cross-language mixing. All wrappers
# share the runtime implementation `MezzeForeign`, so per-type tagging
# is zero-cost at runtime.
#
# Each `<Lang>Foreign` type ships an inherent impl block exposing BOTH
# the language's idiomatic vocabulary AND the universal InteropLibrary
# vocabulary (`invoke_member`, `get_member`, `execute`, `instantiate`,
# `array_at`, `array_size`, `as_int`, `as_str`, `as_float`, `as_bool`,
# `is_null`, `type_name`). Every op routes through the same underlying
# `__native_polyglot_*` primitives — the language-agnostic kernel this
# module registers.
#
# The polyglot Context is managed inside the runtime by the effect
# handler machinery — user code just writes
#   `perform JvmHost in do … let c = Jvm.class { name = "java.util.UUID" } …`
# and never sees the underlying Truffle Context.
use std::result

## Errors returned by every fallible interop operation.
##
## - NotFound       — the requested member / binding does not exist.
## - TypeError      — the guest value did not accept the requested op.
## - GuestError     — the guest language raised an exception; message
##                    and stack are propagated verbatim.
## - ArityMismatch  — execute/invoke arg count mismatched the target.
## - UnsupportedMessage — the underlying Truffle message is not
##                    supported by the receiver.
pub type InteropError = |
  NotFound { name: Str },
  TypeError { message: Str },
  GuestError { message: Str, guest_stack: Str },
  ArityMismatch { expected: Int, actual: Int },
  UnsupportedMessage,
|
